WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.680
<v ->Now let's talk about the AWS Transfer Family.</v>

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.700
So the idea is that you want to send data

3
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.750
in and out of Amazon S3 or EFS,

4
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.740
but you don't wanna use the S3 APIs,

5
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.760
you don't want to use the EFS network file system.

6
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.730
You just wanna use the FTP protocol.

7
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:16.563
In this case,

8
00:00:16.563 --> 00:00:19.940
you need to use the Transfer Family service from AWS.

9
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.040
So it supports three kinds of protocols.

10
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.390
It supports AWS Transfer for FTP.

11
00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.420
So the File Transfer Protocol, FTP.

12
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.360
FTPS, which is the File Transfer Protocol over SSL,

13
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.750
so encrypted form.

14
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.642
Or SFTP, which is a Secure File Transfer Protocol.

15
00:00:35.642 --> 00:00:37.960
Now you don't need to be an expert on those.

16
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.120
Just know that FTP is unencrypted,

17
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.200
whereas FTPS and a SFTP are encrypted in flight.

18
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.040
Now the idea is that using the FTP protocol,

19
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.158
you can upload to S3 or EFS.

20
00:00:48.158 --> 00:00:51.720
The Transfer Family has a fully managed infrastructure.

21
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.650
It's scalable, reliable, and highly available.

22
00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.360
So you'd want to manage at all that capability.

23
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.378
And in terms of pricing,

24
00:00:57.378 --> 00:01:00.410
you're going to pay per provisioned endpoints per hour,

25
00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.941
plus a fee per gigabytes of data transferred

26
00:01:02.941 --> 00:01:06.160
in and out of the Transfer Family.

27
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.090
You can store and manage the users' credentials

28
00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.700
for that service within the service.

29
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.270
Or you can also integrate

30
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.290
with existing authentication system,

31
00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.224
such as Microsoft Active Directory,

32
00:01:15.224 --> 00:01:16.360
LDAP, Okta,

33
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.670
Amazon Cognito, or any custom source.

34
00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.830
The usage of this is you have an FTP interface

35
00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.820
into Amazon S3 or EFS.

36
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.080
So it's to share files,

37
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.160
to share public datasets,

38
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.920
to do CRM, ERP, et cetera, et cetera.

39
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.700
So just a diagram for you to understand.

40
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.100
The Transfer Family has three flavors,

41
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.780
and the users can access directly

42
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.390
using the endpoints of the FTP.

43
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.120
Or optionally, you can use a DNS called Route 53

44
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.590
to provide your own host name

45
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.500
into the FTP service.

46
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.380
And then the FTP service,

47
00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.830
so the transfer for FTP service

48
00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:52.000
will have an IAM Role that will be assumed

49
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.520
to send or receive the files

50
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.940
from Amazon S3 or Amazon EFS.

51
00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:56.890
And this is done transparently.

52
00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.200
You don't have to set many things up.

53
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.260
And finally, if you wanted

54
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.060
to secure the Transfer Family services,

55
00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.150
then you could authenticate your users

56
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.530
using an external authentication system,

57
00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.220
such as Active Directory

58
00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.190
LDAP or all the things that I just said

59
00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.560
in the previous slide, okay?

60
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.930
Finally, you need to learn about the Endpoint Types

61
00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.470
for the Transfer Family as, and their associated security.

62
00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.760
So the Public Endpoint looks like this.

63
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.630
So with the endpoint in our Cloud.

64
00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.890
And then the internet is going to be able

65
00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.550
to access this point.

66
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.840
Now, what is going to happen is that the public IP

67
00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.010
of this endpoint is going to be managed by AWS.

68
00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.130
And that is something that can be changing over time.

69
00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.460
So the IP can change over time.

70
00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.900
And so the recommendation is for you to use the DNS name,

71
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.980
the public DNS name of your endpoint.

72
00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.080
And that means also that with this setup,

73
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.350
you cannot set up allow lists to filter

74
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:50.110
by source IP addresses from the network security.

75
00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.540
You still get security from within the endpoint,

76
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.700
but you cannot have a firewall

77
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.640
that will only allow specific IPs.

78
00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.973
The other option is to use

79
00:02:58.973 --> 00:03:02.170
a VPC Endpoint with Internal Access.

80
00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.930
So in this case, your SFTP, for example

81
00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:08.300
is going to be deployed within your VPC

82
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.930
and your EC2 instances within your VPC

83
00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:15.180
are able to access privately your endpoint

84
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.420
and your Corporate Data Center,

85
00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.060
if connected via VPN or direct connect,

86
00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.490
can also access your internal endpoint.

87
00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:25.000
So here you get static private IPs

88
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.250
to access your endpoints,

89
00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.070
and you can set up allow list

90
00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.900
because you can leverage now the security group feature

91
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.130
or Network ACLs to get access

92
00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.860
and manage access into your endpoint.

93
00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:38.070
So these two make sense

94
00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.870
but you can get a hybrid version,

95
00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.520
called the VPC Endpoint with Internet-Facing Access.

96
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:44.800
So in this case,

97
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.390
while we get the same VPC deploying

98
00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.430
and we can have the EC2 instances within our VPC,

99
00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.760
or any server in our Corporate Data Center

100
00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.090
to access privately our endpoints.

101
00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:58.550
But also we can set up public IPs

102
00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:01.300
for our endpoints using an elastic IP.

103
00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.133
So in this case,

104
00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:04.210
we have full control over the elastic IP

105
00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.210
because we attach it ourselves

106
00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.570
and therefore the internet can access

107
00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.540
again our Endpoint using the elastic IP.

108
00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.730
But because we manage everything,

109
00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:17.180
then we can set up security groups to actually control

110
00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.924
who from the internet can access our SFTP, for example.

111
00:04:20.924 --> 00:04:23.170
So it's very important for you to understand

112
00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:24.330
the different endpoint types.

113
00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.660
The exam will ask you to choose the correct one.

114
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:27.990
And hopefully that makes sense

115
00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.280
from an architectural decision, okay?

116
00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:31.180
So that's it for this lecture,

117
00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:32.013
I hope you liked it

118
00:04:32.013 --> 00:04:33.950
and I will see you in the next lecture.

